# Deliberately no upper bound on pve-manager. A `Breaks: pve-manager (>= 10~)`
# would hold back a legitimate major upgrade of the hypervisor to protect an
# add-on, which is a worse outcome than the one it prevents. proxmod probes
# every seam it uses at runtime and disables itself feature by feature instead.
# See docs/compatibility.md.
# No util-linux here, though proxmod-reapply and proxmodctl both need flock(1)
# from it. util-linux is Essential: yes, so it is present on every Debian system
# by definition and an unversioned dependency on it says nothing — which is what
# lintian's depends-on-essential-package-without-using-version means. The
# requirement is real; the way to record it is this comment, not a field that
# can never be unsatisfied.
Package: proxmod
Architecture: all
Depends:
 ${misc:Depends},
 ${perl:Depends},
 pve-manager,
 libpve-http-server-perl,
# Proxmod::Frontend builds the loader response with HTTP::Headers and
# HTTP::Response. libpve-http-server-perl depends on this too, so the module is
# present on every PVE host today and nothing has ever failed for want of it —
# which is exactly why it needs naming here. A transitive dependency is a
# promise made by somebody else's package about somebody else's reasons, and
# the day pve-http-server stops needing HTTP::Message, proxmod's frontend
# breaks on a host whose dependencies are all satisfied. CI installs it by name
# for the same reason: nothing else in the test environment would drag it in.
 libhttp-message-perl,
 systemd,
Recommends:
 curl,
Suggests:
 proxmod-example-hello,
Description: extension framework for Proxmox VE 9.x
 proxmod lets a Debian package add REST endpoints and web-interface components
 to a Proxmox VE host without modifying a single Proxmox-owned file.
 .
 Extensions are loaded into the running pvedaemon and pveproxy by a systemd
 ExecStart wrapper that adds one Perl module to the daemons' command line. That
 module registers each extension's API methods and injects one script tag into
 the web interface at render time. Nothing is patched, so `dpkg -V pve-manager`
 stays clean and there is nothing to reapply after an upgrade.
 .
 An extension package needs no maintainer scripts: it ships a Perl module, a
 manifest and a JavaScript file, and proxmod's dpkg trigger converges the host.
 .
 Every layer is written to fail toward stock Proxmox. A broken extension is
 isolated to itself, a missing seam disables only the feature that needed it,
 and a daemon that does not come back after a restart causes proxmod to remove
 its own drop-ins and restart it unmodified.
